# Deploy the commit the container asked for. Started by archon-deploy.path.
#
# Runs on the HOST, which is the whole point: the deploy restarts the container
# that asked for it, so the thing doing the work must live outside it.
#
# Watch one: journalctl -u archon-deploy -f

[Unit]
Description=Deploy the Archon commit the container asked for

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/opt/archon/scripts/deploy-on-request.sh

# The same path archon-deploy.path watches, and it must stay the same path:
# this is where the request the unit reacted to actually is. The script can
# discover it from Docker on its own, but being told is cheaper and survives a
# container that was never created. Kept honest by a conformance test rather
# than by remembering — see scripts/deploy-on-request.test.ts.
Environment=VOLUME=/var/lib/docker/volumes/archon_archon_data/_data

# THIS DEADLINE IS THE DEPLOY'S WHOLE BUDGET, and deploy-local.sh spends it:
# a docker build takes minutes and the wait for a turn-gap can take half an
# hour. systemd's default 90s would kill it mid-build.
#
# The two numbers below must be the SAME number, and the script derives every
# internal timeout from the environment one — including how long step 5 may
# wait, which is the budget minus what the swap still needs. They used to be
# independent 1800s values, so a deploy that waited 27m34s for a quiet moment
# swapped the container and was killed 108s later while the new image booted.
# The swap had happened; no verdict was written anywhere. A wait that can
# consume the entire deadline leaves nothing to report with.
TimeoutStartSec=3600
Environment=DEPLOY_BUDGET_SECONDS=3600
